porting set on $\\Omega$, and in the growth bound $\\|\\partial V(u)\\|$ means its minimum element norm, equivalently the bound requires a supporting vector of norm at most $C_1+C_2r$ at every $u\\in Q_r$. No bound is imposed on the domain normal cone. The source Assumptions 2.1 use the stronger maximal bound for the ambient potential subgradients before adding the domain indicator; that original bound implies the bounded-selection condition used here. Taking a supremum over the relative-domain supporting set would instead include unbounded normal vectors at proper-domain boundaries and would incorrectly exclude the constrained branch. All original source hypotheses and conclusions are retained by this sufficient, weaker growth convention. For a finite nonnegative Borel measure $\\mu$ on $\\mathbb R^d$, set\n$$F(\\mu)=R\\!\\left(\\int_\\Omega\\Phi(u)\\,d\\mu(u)\\right)+\\int_\\Omega V(u)\\,d\\mu(u)$$\nwhen $\\mu$ is concentrated on $\\Omega$, the Bochner integral exists, and the scalar integral is finite; set $F(\\mu)=+\\infty$ otherwise. For $\\mu\\in\\mathcal P_2(\\Omega)$ with finite $F(\\mu)$, write $m_\\mu=\\int_\\Omega\\Phi\\,d\\mu$ and define its first-variation potential on $\\Omega$ by\n$$F'(\\mu)(u)=dR_{m_\\mu}[\\Phi(u)]+V(u).$$\nNo convexity of $R$ is required by this definition.",
